    doi = {10.51386/25815946/ijsms-v8i2p111},
    publisher = {Science Research Publication},
    year = {2025},
    pages = {94–98},
    abstract = {Socia media encompasses a range of digital tools and platforms, such as Facebook, Instagram, Twitter, and TikTok that enable users to create, share, and interact with content in real-time.This research explored how student usesocial media, focusing on their emotional regulation, social connections, and behavioral tendencies.The mainobjective was to identifysignificantrelationshipbetween the students’ engagement with social mediaand theiremotional regulation, social interactions, and behavioral patterns. Adescriptive-correlational research designwas utilized to collect data for this investigation. The study involved the96grade 7students from Litte Baguio National High School in San Fernando Bukidnon. An adapted survey questionnairewas used for data collection. Statistical analyses were used to calculate the mean and Pearson product-moment correlation coefficient (ρ). The result indicated no significant correlation between thestudents’ social media usage and their emotional regulation, social relationship, and behavioral patterns as evidenced by anr- value of −.04 and ap-value .713, which exceeds the0.05 significance threshold, leading to the acceptance of the null hypothesis.This suggests no substantial connection exists between the students’ social media usage and their emotional regulation, social responsibility, and behavioral patterns because of the remote location with no stable internet connection. The study concludes that social media does not significantly impact these developmental aspects. However, social media remains important for fostering student connections and maintaining relationship with peers, family, and classmates.},
